Reticulum in Haskell

A Reticulum node: wire format, identity and link cryptography,
destinations, transport, resources, interfaces.

Compatible means identical to python-rns 1.4.2 at commit b48b96e6,
openssl backend. The corpus in CORPUS is what says so, and the count
below is the whole claim.


Where it stands

    104 passed, 0 failed, corpus 72746484

Every kind the corpus holds, and the round trip behind the 88 vectors
whose expect holds every byte of raw.


Build

Nix is the only thing that has to be installed.

    nix develop
    cabal build
    ./check

The shell carries the compiler, cabal, and the C compiler and POSIX
tools the corpus builds itself with. Flake.nix names the compiler and
flake.lock pins the package set.


check

    ./check                                 clone the pin and measure
    CORPUS_DIR=../reticulum-vectors ./check use that checkout as it is

CORPUS holds two lines and is the only place they are written:

    commit        the corpus commit to measure against
    fingerprint   the corpus number printed on the result line

A count is a count against one corpus. The fingerprint says which one,
so check refuses a result that carries another.

    exit 0    every vector that was not skipped passed
    exit 1    a vector failed
    exit 2    nothing was measured, or the fingerprint is not the one
              on file


Layout

    src/Reticulum/     the library: the codec is pure, the node is IO
    cmd/harness/       the corpus harness, linked to ./harness by check
    cmd/node/          a node on TCP interfaces, dialled and answered
    test/              what the corpus cannot hold
    reference/         this node with python-rns nodes around it
    .github/workflows/ one job, and it runs ./check
    CLAUDE.md          the rules this repository is written under
    CORPUS             the pin
    DEPENDS            every dependency past base and the crypto library,
                       and what needs it


The harness

One program taking the arguments the corpus's cmd/dump takes:

    ./harness <kind> <rawfile>       fields out
    ./harness -e <kind> <expectfile> raw back

It prints one field per line from this implementation's own code. A
field this implementation does not compute is a dash; the harness
never computes one on the library's behalf. A kind not implemented
exits 77. The contract is the corpus's doc/harness.

Going the other way, the fields are read back into the library's own
types and the library writes the bytes. The keys never enter into it:
a token whose hmac was altered is still a token, which is why such a
vector can be rebuilt at all.


The node

    node [-t] [-l <port>] [-n <name>] [-d <hex>] [-k <file>]
         [<host>:<port> ...]

    -t          be a transport node
    -l <port>   listen, and take every connection as an interface
    -n <name>   hold this destination, and announce it once
    -d <hex>    open a link to this destination and speak on it
    -k <file>   the identity, written if the file is not there

Dials every peer given and answers on the port given, and prints a line
for every announce it takes into its path table: the destination, the
hops it is away, the interface it came on, and the app data. It needs
at least one of the two, a peer or a port. Log lines go to standard
error.

With -t the node is a transport node: it carries announces onward, a
packet naming it as the next hop goes on toward the destination its
path table knows, and a path request it can answer is answered from
the announce it kept. A proof for a packet it passed on goes back the
way that packet came, and a link opened across it keeps running.
Without -t the node listens, answers path requests for its own
destination, and forwards nothing.

The path table is what an announce leaves behind. A shorter path is
taken only when it was emitted later than the one on file, and an
announce the table keeps nothing of is one nothing above it hears
about.

A resource longer than a megabyte goes over a segment at a time. Each
segment is advertised, handed over and proved on its own, the next one
follows the proof of the one before it, and the far end puts them back
together under the hash the first went out with.

A destination the node holds is a destination it answers for. A packet
addressed to it is opened and proved. A link request is answered, and
on the link that opens, a packet is taken and proved, a keepalive is
answered, an identify is remembered, and a close ends it. A resource is
taken part by part, checked against the hash it was advertised under
and proved. Two request paths are served: echo answers with what it was
asked, and length with how many bytes that was, which is the answer a
request too long for one packet gets.

With -d the node is the end that opens. It asks for the path until it
has one, opens a link to that destination, and on it sends a packet and
waits for the proof, asks echo and takes the answer, asks length with
more than one packet holds so the request goes as a resource, and hands
a resource over and waits for the proof of it, and then closes the
link. Every one of those prints a line.

A link is kept while it is used. The end that opened one writes a
keepalive when nothing has crossed it either way for six minutes, and
the other end answers. Either end writes a close, and the end that
takes one keeps nothing of the link. A link nothing has come in on for
twelve minutes is closed the same way, and so is one whose interface is
gone.


Tests

    cabal test

There are none here that a vector already proves. What belongs here is
what the corpus cannot hold: properties, and the layers above link.
Today that is the transport node and what either end of a link does:
the rules the tables follow, the token in the direction that encrypts,
and nodes wired to each other in memory, one carrying what another
announced, opening a link across it, proving what crossed, answering a
link, a request and a resource of its own, and opening a link of its
own to send a request, hand a resource over, hand over one longer than
a segment, and close the link from either end.

    ./reference/check

is the same node between two python-rns nodes that cannot reach each
other any other way, a third that talks to the node itself, and a
fourth the node talks to. It needs python-rns importable, takes about
three minutes, and is not run in CI. Thirty-one lines are checked.
Through the node: it hears the announce, the far side has no path and
then has one two hops away, a packet across it is proved, a link opens,
and what is said over that link arrives. To the node: a path it answers
for itself, a packet it proves, a link it answers, a packet on that
link it proves, a request it serves, a request too long for one packet
that arrives as a resource and is answered from it, an answer as long
as what was asked that goes back as one, a resource it takes whole, one
past a segment that it takes as two and answers as one, and the close
that ends the link, which it takes as the end of it. From the node: a
link it opens, a packet on it that is proved, an answer to a request it
sent, a request of its own too long for one packet, a resource it hands
over and sees proved, one past a segment that goes over as two, and the
close it writes when there is nothing left to say, which the far side
takes as the end.


